<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert the Strong</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/866">866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robertians">Robertians</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert the Strong was a Frankish count and margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a>, charged with defending the lands between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a>. He was killed fighting them at Brissarthe in 866. His sons Odo and Robert I both became kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>, and he was the ancestor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robertians">Robertians</a> and of the Capetian kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-the-strong.jpg" alt="Robert the Strong" width="245" height="236" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%A9n%C3%A9alogie_des_rois_de_France_-_Besan%C3%A7on_Ms677_f139v_Robert_le_Fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Robert's parentage is obscure. One proposal makes him a son of Robert III of Worms; others, based mainly on the recurrence of the name, point to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesbaye">Hesbaye</a> region of present-day eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> or to the family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chrodegang-of-metz">Chrodegang of Metz</a>, though these are unproven. According to the Worms proposal, the family emigrated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> in the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bald">Charles the Bald</a> rewarded them for leaving his enemy by giving Robert the lay abbacy of Marmoutier in 852.</p><p>In 853 Charles made him missus dominicus in Maine, Anjou and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/touraine">Touraine</a>, giving him de facto control of the old ducatus Cenomannicus, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a>. His rise came at the expense of the established family of the Rorigonids and was meant to curb their regional power and to defend <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> and Breton raids.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 858 Robert joined a rebellion against Charles. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bretons">Bretons</a> under Salomon he led the Neustrian nobles and invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>. The revolt followed a marriage alliance between Charles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erispoe">Erispoe</a>, King of Brittany, and the investment of Charles's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-stammerer">Louis the Stammerer</a> with the regnum Neustriae, which curtailed the powers of both Salomon and Robert. In 861 Charles made peace with Robert and appointed him Count of Anjou, and Robert then defended the northern coast against a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> invasion.</p><p>In 862 two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> fleets converged on Brittany, and Salomon hired one to ravage the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a> valley. Robert captured twelve of its ships and then hired the former Seine Vikings, for 6,000 pounds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, to attack Salomon's realm. Peace did not last: when Salomon made peace in 863 the Vikings, deprived of enemy lands to loot, ravaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a>. Charles made Robert lay abbot of Saint Martin at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tours">Tours</a>. He also fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-ii-of-aquitaine">Pepin II of Aquitaine</a> and defended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autun">Autun</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> in 856 and 863.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 2 July 866 Robert was killed at the Battle of Brissarthe, defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a> against a joint Breton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raiding party led by Salomon and the Viking chieftain Hastein. The Viking commander was trapped in a nearby church, and Robert took off his armour to besiege it. The Vikings launched a surprise attack and he died in the melee. The Annales Fuldenses called him a second Maccabaeus. He left a nine-year-old son, Odo, later king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> (888–898); his younger son Robert I reigned in 922–923. His descendants are called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robertians">Robertians</a>, and through his great-grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a> he is the ancestor of all the Capetians.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert the Strong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A joint Breton-Viking army defeated the West Franks near Brissarthe in Neustria, killing the Neustrian margrave <b>Robert the Strong</b> and Ranulf I, Duke of Aquitaine.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-2">2 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Robertian family had led the defence of Neustria against the Vikings since <b>Robert the Strong</b> in the 860s, and two of its members, Odo and Robert I, had briefly been kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-the-franks">Duchy of the Franks</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Brissarthe, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.7,-0.45&amp;year=856" aria-label="Brissarthe on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4539 492 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Robert the Strong"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4989 762h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4989 762h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.7,-0.45&amp;year=856">What was here in 856 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
